"[2511.01386v1] RAGSmith: A Framework for Finding the Optimal Composition of Retrieval-Augmented Generation Methods Across Datasets (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.01386v1)\nciteturn2233view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2232view4\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 3 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:RAGSmith: A Framework for Finding the Optimal Composition of Retrieval-Augmented Generation Methods Across Datasets\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Muhammed Yusuf Kartal (1), cite6†Suha Kagan Kose (2), cite7†Korhan Sevinç (1), cite8†Burak Aktas (2) ((1) TOBB University of Economics and Technology, (2) Roketsan Inc.)\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled RAGSmith: A Framework for Finding the Optimal Composition of Retrieval-Augmented Generation Methods Across Datasets, by Muhammed Yusuf Kartal (1) and 4 other authors\nL14: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) quality depends on many interacting choices across retrieval, ranking, augmentation, prompting, and generation, so optimizing modules in isolation is brittle. We introduce RAGSmith, a modular framework that treats RAG design as an end-to-end architecture search over nine technique families and 46{,}080 feasible pipeline configurations.\nL16: A genetic search optimizes a scalar objective that jointly aggregates retrieval metrics (recall@k, mAP, nDCG, MRR) and generation metrics (LLM-Judge and semantic similarity). We evaluate on six Wikipedia-derived domains (Mathematics, Law, Finance, Medicine, Defense Industry, Computer Science), each with 100 questions spanning factual, interpretation, and long-answer types.\nL17: RAGSmith finds configurations that consistently outperform naive RAG baseline by +3.8\\% on average (range +1.2\\% to +6.9\\% across domains), with gains up to +12.5\\% in retrieval and +7.5\\% in generation.\nL18: The search typically explores $\\approx 0.2\\%$ of the space ($\\sim 100$ candidates) and discovers a robust backbone -- vector retrieval plus post-generation reflection/revision -- augmented by domain-dependent choices in expansion, reranking, augmentation, and prompt reordering; passage compression is never selected. Improvement magnitude correlates with question type, with larger gains on factual/long-answer mixes than interpretation-heavy sets.\nL19: These results provide practical, domain-aware guidance for assembling effective RAG systems and demonstrate the utility of evolutionary search for full-pipeline optimization.\nL20: Comments:  | 45 pages\nL21: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\nL22: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | H.3.3; I.2.7\nL23: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2511.01386 [cs.CL]\nL24:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2511.01386v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL25:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.01386†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Muhammed Yusuf Kartal [cite14†view email ]\nL29: [v1] Mon, 3 Nov 2025 09:36:27 UTC (1,050 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled RAGSmith: A Framework for Finding the Optimal Composition of Retrieval-Augmented Generation Methods Across Datasets, by Muhammed Yusuf Kartal (1) and 4 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite9†View PDF L38:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite15†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite16†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CL\nL45: \nL46: cite17†< prev |   cite18†ne