from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.01059v1] Efficient Test-Time Retrieval Augmented Generation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.01059v1)\nciteturn2233view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2232view5\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 2 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:Efficient Test-Time Retrieval Augmented Generation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Hailong Yin , cite6†Bin Zhu , cite7†Jingjing Chen , cite8†Chong-Wah Ngo L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Efficient Test-Time Retrieval Augmented Generation, by Hailong Yin and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Although Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate significant capabilities, their reliance on parametric knowledge often leads to inaccuracies. Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) mitigates this by incorporating external knowledge, but these methods may introduce irrelevant retrieved documents, leading to inaccurate responses.\nL17: While the integration methods filter out incorrect answers from multiple responses, but lack external knowledge like RAG methods, and their high costs require balancing overhead with performance gains. To address these issues, we propose an Efficient Test-Time Retrieval-Augmented Generation Framework named ET2RAG to improve the performance of LLMs while maintaining efficiency.\nL18: Specifically, ET2RAG is a training-free method, that first retrieves the most relevant documents and augments the LLMs to efficiently generate diverse candidate responses by managing response length. Then we compute the similarity of candidate responses and employ a majority voting mechanism to select the most suitable response as the final output.\nL19: In particular, we discover that partial generation is sufficient to capture the key information necessary for consensus calculation, allowing us to effectively perform majority voting without the need for fully generated responses. Thus, we can reach a balance between computational cost and performance by managing the response length for the number of retrieved documents for majority voting.\nL20: Experimental results demonstrate that ET2RAG significantly enhances performance across three tasks, including open-domain question answering, recipe generation and image captioning.\nL21: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2511.01059 [cs.AI]\nL23:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2511.01059v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL24:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.01059†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Hailong Yin [cite14†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Sun, 2 Nov 2025 19:32:39 UTC (30,320 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Efficient Test-Time Retrieval Augmented Generation, by Hailong Yin and 3 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite9†View PDF L37:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite15†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite16†view license†creativecommons.org L41: \nL42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.AI\nL45: \nL46: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L47: \nL48: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2025-11 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite22†cs L53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## Bi